<h3>The Question</h3>
<p>
    <a href="/backlog/3400-the-ratified-hosted-key-billed-delivery-mode-has-no-metering/">Backlog #3400</a>:
    <a href="/docs/agent/platform-decisions.md#operations-declared-once-callers-generated">clause 4</a> of the
    operation-engine statute (ratified #3031) names two <strong>permanent</strong> delivery modes —
    solo-local (subscription-funded, the operator's own machine) and hosted-key-billed (behind the same
    seam, billed per key) — as "two permanent products for different people, not a migration". The solo mode
    is built. The hosted mode has zero design past its name: no card describes <strong>attribution</strong>
    (whose key, which run), <strong>metering</strong> (what unit, read from where), or an
    <strong>auth</strong> story for
    <code>scripts/operations/http-adapter.mjs</code> — which today has no auth/token concept at all
    (grepped for <code>localhost|auth|token|apiKey|bearer</code>: the only hits are unrelated doc prose) and
    is mounted only into <code>plateau-app:tools/dev-panel/vite-plugin.ts</code>, an implicitly-trusted
    localhost dev panel. This survey grounds the design — not the build — of the three missing pieces
    against comparable metered, key-authenticated HTTP APIs.
</p>

<h3>Recommendation (to ratify in #3400)</h3>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Per-run dollar metering already exists and is already engine-level</strong> — see Finding 4,
        added after a skeptic sub-agent pass found the original "metering is greenfield" framing false.
        <code>judge</code>-step cost is already tracked, automatically, caller-agnostically. The only real
        gap is <code>callerId</code> attribution on that existing telemetry, plus cost capture for the one
        effect kind with none today (<code>dispatch: true</code>, an agent spawn) — both still engine-level,
        never a per-operation opt-in declaration field, for the same forced-invariant reason
        <a href="/backlog/2779-reliable-per-build-cost-metering-attribution-model-tier-poli/">#2779</a> (a
        <em>plateau-app</em>-scoped sibling card, different consumer) names an opt-in scheme as an
        under-billing risk.</li>
    <li><strong>Auth is a bearer API key</strong>, checked by the HTTP transport adapter ahead of the derived
        route table — never OAuth/end-user login (this caller shape is machine-to-machine, not an end-user
        session) and never a per-operation declaration field (would repeat the exact "hand-written route for
        something that could be declared once" defect clause 1 of the same statute already forbids, applied
        to auth instead of routing).</li>
    <li><strong>Attribution is a corollary, not a third fork</strong> — once auth resolves a
        <code>callerId</code> and metering threads that <code>callerId</code> onto the run's existing
        judge-cost telemetry (and, for a dispatch effect, its resolved cost — see Finding 4, not a discrete
        metering-event ledger), "whose key, which run" is answered by composing the two defaults above; no
        separate design choice remains.</li>
    <li><strong>Building any of this is NOT-YET</strong> — gated on a concrete trigger (the run-store
        becoming a shared, cross-actor store per the still-open
        <a href="/backlog/2626-operational-state-store-session-local-sidecars-now-a-shared-/">#2626</a> /
        <a href="/backlog/2742-un-gate-stand-up-the-shared-durable-operational-state-store-/">#2742</a>
        migration, or a caller of the HTTP adapter that is not the operator's own co-located session),
        mirroring how #2626 and
        <a href="/backlog/3049-the-conveyor-as-a-shippable-product-not-machinery-and-what-t/">#3049</a>
        both dissolve a "build now vs later" question to accepted-on-merit + a named trigger rather than a
        ratifiable fork.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Finding 1 — 2026 metered billing converges on backing-meter-per-price, idempotent event reporting</h3>
<p>
    <strong>Superseded in shape by Finding 4 below — read this finding for the billing-industry pattern only,
    not for how #3400 actually implements metering.</strong> Stripe changed metered billing for 2026: since
    API version <code>2025-03-31.basil</code> the legacy usage records API is retired and every metered price
    requires a backing <strong>Meter</strong>. The pattern: create a meter, attach a metered price to a
    subscription, report usage <strong>events</strong> at usage time with an <strong>idempotency key</strong>,
    and let the billing platform aggregate events into an invoice at cycle end. The shape that matters for
    this decision is not Stripe's API surface (a plateau-app-scoped concern, per #2779/#2780) — it is the
    <strong>event shape</strong>: a metered fact is reported as a discrete, idempotent fact at the moment work
    happens, never inferred after the fact from a log. This finding originally proposed mapping that onto the
    operation engine's <code>effect</code> step kind as a new, generic per-effect ledger event — <strong>Finding
    4 corrects this</strong>: a skeptic sub-agent pass found real per-run cost telemetry already ships for
    <code>judge</code> steps, so the idempotent-event-at-usage-time <em>principle</em> survives, but the
    concrete shape #3400 actually ratifies is a <code>callerId</code>/<code>costUsd</code> field addition to
    that existing telemetry (plus a <code>resolveInFlight</code>-keyed capture for <code>dispatch</code>
    effects), not a new, separate metering-event ledger.
</p>

<h3>Finding 2 — every surveyed multi-tenant HTTP API resolves the caller by API key, not per-request identity</h3>
<p>
    AWS API Gateway's usage-plan model: a custom API key <em>is</em> the tenant identifier — the gateway
    evaluates policy and looks up the usage plan by the key on every request, and a per-request DynamoDB
    write plus a Lambda billing glue layer ties usage to Stripe. Cloudflare's own Workers for Platforms
    (the constellation's own substrate, already leaned on by <a
    href="/backlog/2626-operational-state-store-session-local-sidecars-now-a-shared-/">#2626</a>'s DO/D1
    ruling) states the identical pattern in its own terms: "meter every request for billing," with
    per-tenant limits enforced by a dispatch layer in front of the tenant's own code, authenticated by a
    scoped API token. Anthropic's and OpenAI's own consumer APIs — the closest analog to "an AI-development
    engine billed per call" — use a bearer API key in an <code>Authorization</code>/<code>x-api-key</code>
    header, never OAuth, for exactly this caller shape: a programmatic, account-level credential, not an
    end-user session. The multi-tenant literature is explicit about <em>why</em> OAuth is the wrong tool
    here: "many multi-tenant platforms end up using both: API keys for backend integrations and internal
    automation, OAuth for anything acting on behalf of an individual user... the mistake to avoid is
    defaulting to whichever one was easiest to implement first, and then stretching it to cover use cases it
    was never designed for." The operation engine's callers include the console
    (<code>plateau-app:tools/dev-panel</code>) — a browser UI a person operates, not purely
    machine-to-machine — but the credential it authenticates is still the <em>account's</em> key, not a
    per-person login, since <code>key-billed</code> bills a key/account rather than a person; OAuth's
    delegation/consent model has no third party to delegate to here, so the API-key branch is still the one
    every comparable system converges on.
</p>

<h3>Finding 3 — key storage and entropy, so the default has a concrete shape when it is eventually built</h3>
<p>
    Convergent guidance across the surveyed sources: generate keys with enough entropy that brute-forcing is
    unrealistic (a random 256-bit value, encoded as a string, is a reasonable baseline), store keys as hashed
    values rather than plaintext — the same discipline as a password — and record which tenant/account a key
    was issued for so the lookup on use is a simple table read, not a live identity-provider round trip. This
    is recorded here as the shape the eventual build would take; nothing here is built by this decision.
</p>

<h3>Finding 4 — corrected in-session by a skeptic sub-agent pass: per-run dollar metering already ships</h3>
<p>
    The first-drafted design (above, before correction) proposed metering as if it were wholly greenfield,
    wrapping a new generic hook around every applied effect. A skeptic sub-agent pass, checking the claim
    against the WE tree, found this false: <code>TELEMETRY_NUMBERS = ['costUsd', 'durationMs', 'wallMs',
    'numTurns', 'loadedContextTokens']</code> (<code>scripts/operations/run-record.mjs:109</code>) is already
    populated from every <code>judge</code>-step spawn's own reported cost
    (<code>scripts/lib/judge-spawn.mjs:642,676</code>), already packaged by <code>createDefaultJudge</code>'s
    judge wrapper (<code>scripts/operations/cli-adapter.mjs:488</code>) and threaded onto the run by
    <code>driveRun</code> (<code>scripts/operations/cli-adapter.mjs:571</code>) — the one shared path every
    real caller funnels through — and already printed as <code>judge spend: $X.XXXX over N juror(s)</code>
    (<code>scripts/operations/cli-adapter.mjs:792</code>). Metering-by-dollars for <code>judge</code> steps is
    shipped, not an open question. The one real gap is the effect kind with zero cost capture today —
    <code>dispatch: true</code>, an agent spawn (<code>scripts/operations/effect-executor.mjs:87</code>) —
    whose cost is not known at the moment the effect is applied (it returns an <code>inFlight</code> marker,
    <code>:114</code>, and reports back later). That reshapes the default: thread <code>callerId</code> onto
    the existing telemetry (a small addition), and capture dispatch cost specifically at
    <code>resolveInFlight</code> (<code>:411</code>, the only supported way an in-flight dispatch closes out)
    rather than at the generic effect-apply choke point. This finding is the canonical illustration of why
    prep runs prior-art research against the <em>real tree</em>, not just comparable external systems — an
    external survey alone would never have surfaced that half the target already ships.
</p>

